{name:"Why MCP makes local development feel connected",eyebrow:"TOOLS & WORKFLOWS",summary:"MCP gives an assistant a clear, inspectable way to work with the tools you already use.",image:load("postOneImage"),credit:"Photo by Chris Ried on Unsplash",body:`<p>A good development tool should meet you where the work already lives. Model Context Protocol (MCP) does exactly that: it gives an assistant a shared way to discover tools, read useful context, and request actions through a defined interface.</p><h2>One protocol, many useful tools</h2><p>Without a common protocol, every new integration needs its own custom bridge. MCP replaces that repeated wiring with a consistent tool contract. A server can describe what it offers, the inputs it expects, and the results it returns. The assistant can then use those capabilities in the context of a real task.</p><p>This blog is a concrete example. The local Umbraco MCP server let us inspect document types, configure a block grid, import media, and publish pages. Each action had a purpose and a result we could read back. The work happened in the CMS, so editors can keep using the familiar backoffice afterward.</p><h2>Context makes the difference</h2><p>Having a tool is useful; knowing when and how to use it is better. MCP makes it possible to pair tool descriptions with project conventions and current content. An assistant can see the available schema before writing a document, use the right media reference, and verify that a block payload actually saved.</p><p>The result is less time translating between systems and more time shaping the experience people will read. MCP is exciting because it turns isolated capabilities into a practical, reviewable workflow.</p>`},
{name:"MCP is awesome when people stay in control",eyebrow:"BUILDING WITH CONFIDENCE",summary:"The best MCP workflows combine automation, transparent changes, and room for human judgment.",image:load("postTwoImage"),credit:"Photo by Vardan Papikyan on Unsplash",body:`<p>It is easy to imagine an assistant doing more with tools. The more interesting question is how that work remains understandable. MCP helps by making capabilities explicit: a server offers named operations, structured inputs, and responses that can be inspected.</p><h2>Small steps are easier to trust</h2><p>Consider publishing a blog. We can first examine the existing content tree, then create reusable block types, configure a block grid, add images to a media folder, and finally publish pages. Reading the result after each structural change catches mismatched keys and missing values early.</p><p>That stepwise approach is useful well beyond content management. A team can connect local tools for design, documentation, issue tracking, or testing, while still deciding which operations belong in an automated flow and which decisions need a person.</p><h2>Reusable structure opens creative space</h2><p>Blocks are a good match for this way of working. Editors can rearrange a hero, an article, or a list of posts without rebuilding the site. Developers keep one rendering path and one set of components. The assistant can create content through the same schema the backoffice understands.</p><p>MCP is awesome because it makes that collaboration tangible. The protocol does not supply taste or judgment by itself; it makes the right tools available at the right moment, with enough structure to check the outcome.</p>`}
